###START EXAMPLE 2###
---QUESTION---
The polytene chromosomes seen in Drosophila silvarentis with the aid of a light microscope are?

A) not actively carrying out transcription
B) produced only during mitosis
C) produced by repeated rounds of DNA replication followed by nuclear division
D) multiple copies of a single chromosome

---ANSWER---
Polytene chromosomes form when DNA replicates multiple times without cell division, creating many parallel copies of the same chromosome that remain synapsed together. This results in giant chromosomes with multiple DNA strands of the same chromosome aligned side-by-side, which is why they're visible under light microscopy.
ANSWER: D
###END EXAMPLE 2###

